// LED Mapper wire protocol (design doc §7) — protocol-buffer contract.
//
// This is the WIRE format for all host <-> phone communication: binary
// protobuf frames over the /ws WebSocket, wrapped in the ClientMessage /
// ServerMessage envelopes (one oneof arm per §7 message type).
//
// JSON parity: field names and shapes are chosen so the proto3 JSON mapping
// of every message matches the pre-protobuf JSON wire exactly (the server's
// internal models and the web app's TypeScript types predate this file and
// are generated from the JSON Schemas in ../schemas/). That parity is what
// keeps the migration boundary thin — each end converts envelope <-> flat
// JSON object at the socket and nothing else changes. Notes:
//   - vectors are FLAT `repeated double` (JSON: [x, y, z]), matching the
//     schema's fixed-arity arrays; `K` carries an explicit json_name;
//   - enum-like fields (encoding, fec, units, frame, syncPattern) are
//     STRINGS, because the JSON wire uses "gray-hue"-style values that
//     proto enum JSON names cannot reproduce;
//   - nullable-on-the-wire fields are `optional` (unset <-> null);
//   - trajectories are `repeated Vec3` and need a tiny shape conversion at
//     the boundary ([[x,y,z], ...] <-> [{v: [x,y,z]}, ...]) — the one spot
//     proto3 JSON cannot express nested arrays. Segment polylines (Topology)
//     get the same conversion.
//
// PLAYER PROFILES (docs/esp32-led-mapping-plan.md): two player targets
// implement this protocol behind one wire. Which envelope arms each supports:
//
//   CORE (every player — the stable player protocol):
//     client: hello, time_sync_ping, start_mapping, configure, stop_mapping,
//             submit_map, get_pattern, set_counting_pattern, set_led_count,
//             submit_topology, set_playback, get_playback
//     server: welcome, time_sync_pong, mapping_started, mapping_stopped,
//             pattern_state, result_ready, error, counting_state,
//             led_count_state, playback_state
//
//   PI ONLY (host-solve + live-map + telemetry paths — the Pi serves the
//   webapp and can run the solver):
//     client: detections, imu_batch, exposure_report, get_status,
//             get_live_map, get_solve_status
//     server: status, live_map, solve_status
//     The Pi's welcome carries solverBenchMs; an ESP32 never sets it, which
//     already makes the phone's placement decision "solve on phone"
//     (web/src/solver/placement.ts).
//
//   Unknown/unsupported arms on a player MUST produce a bounded reply, never
//   a crash: REQUEST arms (those with a defined reply) get
//   error{code: "unsupported"}; fire-and-forget arms (detections, imu_batch,
//   exposure_report) MAY be silently dropped so a chatty phone cannot flood
//   the error channel.

